            <ArticleTitle>Lung age in women with morbid obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>265-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ramb.2012.12.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0104-4230(13)00063-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the influence of morbid obesity on the lung age in women and to correlate with body mass, body mass index (BMI), and ventilatory variables.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This was a cross-sectional study with 72 morbidly obese women and a control group consisting of 37 normal weight women. The subjects performed a pulmonary function test to determine lung age, and the results were correlated to anthropometric variables and lung volumes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The morbidly obese group had significantly higher lung age (50.1±6.8 years) than the control group (38.8±11.4 years). There was no difference in chronological age between groups. There was a significant positive correlation among chronological age, body mass, BMI, and lung age (r=0.3647, 0.4182, and 0.3743, respectively). There was a negative correlation among forced vital capacity (FVC), forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV1), FEV1/FVC ratio, expiratory reserve volume (ERV), and lung age (r=-0.7565, -0.8769, --0.2723, and -0.2417, respectively).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Lung age is increased in morbidly obese women and is associated with increased body mass and BMI.</AbstractText>
